<% if game_entry %>
  <% case game_entry.status %>
  <% when "accepted" %>
    <%= link_to t("shared.current_games_status.play"), show_current_level_path(game_id: game.id) %>
  <% else %>
    <%= t("shared.current_games_status.not_registered") %>
  <% end %>
<%# A gated game never creates a GameEntry, so game_entry is nil here rather
    than carrying some third status -- this is the whole reason it's a
    separate branch. That invariant is enforced now, by
    GameEntriesController#ensure_game_is_not_gated; it was only ever ASSERTED
    here, and until that filter existed the dashboard offered "Подать заявку"
    on gated games and every branch below was simply skipped for a team who
    had used it.

    A caller must therefore pass game_entry nil for a gated game rather than
    whatever GameEntry.of returns: an entry accepted before an operator
    flipped the game's access_mode still exists, and this branch order would
    hand it the play link with no pass behind it.

    gated_live is the caller's job, not this partial's: it is computed once
    per listing (not per row) from the team's access_passes, exactly the shape
    GamesHelper#game_team_counts already uses for the scheduled columns beside
    this one, and passing it in is what keeps this partial from re-querying
    per row and reintroducing the N+1 games_listing_spec.rb pins.
    local_assigns, not a bare reference, so a caller that never passes it
    defaults to "no access" rather than raising -- but every caller in the
    app does pass it, and a gated row is reachable from all of them: a gated
    game has no schedule, so whether it counts as `started?` is decided by
    whatever starts_at happens to hold. %>
<% elsif game.pass_required? %>
  <% if local_assigns[:gated_live] %>
    <%= link_to t("shared.current_games_status.play"), show_current_level_path(game_id: game.id) %>
  <% else %>
    <%= link_to t("shared.current_games_status.redeem_code_link"), redeem_access_code_path %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
